as an independent filmmaker it means  everything in the world to be part of  presenting Charles Burnett with non re  Oscar he's a true hero of the cinematic  arts whose work continues to inspire and  influence I'm I'm very aware that in  speaking about someone who has battled  against cliches in all his films I'm  resorting to one when I say that in  regards to Charles Burnett's work the  cream rises it can sometimes take years  sometimes decades but eventually the  best will stand the test of time and  rise to the top it has taken 40 years  for killer of sheep to become  acknowledged the acknowledged  masterpiece that it is today it kept  rising steadily until it demanded our  attention some of the resistance comes  from the fact that it's not an easy film  for example there is no conventional  three-act structure but Charles knows  that life doesn't have a three-act  structure and nor should a film about  life killer of sheep is a lyrical  portrait of a community on the margins  the film doesn't ask us to feel sorry  for its characters to pity them but it  does demand that we afford them dignity  and respect this demand comes as a  whisper not a shout it comes by allowing  the audience to spend quality time with  the film's characters the quiet everyday  moments the time in-between I first  became aware of Charles Burnett's work  when I was a film student at NYU  Charles was telling stories of the  working class and using first-time  actors and shooting on location I found  this particular approach the  storytelling and craft which was very  similar to that used by the italian  neorealists deeply inspiring and right  now there is a new wave of American  filmmakers from all backgrounds who  regard their work as a political and  social act this wave of artistic  activism can be directly traced back to  Burnett's groundbreaking debut in  speaking about his lead character Stan  Charles has said that Stan's real  problems lie within the family trying to  make that work